\begin{frontmatter}

\title{Conformal Perceptual Shielding for Adaptive Bitrate Streaming}

\author[a]{Saeed Zarbi}
\author[a]{Leili Farzinvash\corref{c1}}
\ead{l.farzinvash@tabrizu.ac.ir}
\author[a]{Pedram Salehpour}
\cortext[c1]{Corresponding author.}

\address[a]{Department of Computer Engineering, Faculty of Electrical and Computer Engineering, University of Tabriz, Tabriz, Iran}

\begin{abstract}
Client-side Adaptive Bitrate (ABR) controllers select, for each video chunk, one rung of the bitrate ladder to request.
That request can stall playback when throughput drops, or waste bandwidth when top rungs are perceptually saturated, harming Quality of Experience (QoE).
Prior solutions for these challenges ignored perceptual saturation and offered no coverage guarantee.
Here, coverage refers to the probability that the actual throughput of the next chunk is at least as high as the lower bound used to determine whether the download can be completed before the buffer is empty.
We propose the Certified Perceptual Shield (CPS), a model-agnostic wrapper for heuristic, model-based, or learned ABR policies.
CPS, which has three components, works as follows.
First, knee-based bandwidth banking lowers even a feasible proposal to the smallest rung whose Video Multimethod Assessment Fusion (VMAF) score lies within a perceptual budget \(\varepsilon\) of the proposal.
Second, an online split-conformal throughput lower bound supplies that coverage without a parametric traffic model.
Third, a feasibility check admits only rungs whose download time under that bound fits the buffer margin; otherwise CPS emits the lowest rung.
This yields a per-chunk probabilistic stall certificate, not a zero-stall promise.
An optional predictive mode (CPS-P) widens \(\varepsilon\) only when the buffer is low or a short look-ahead forecasts a dip.
On \(\CPSepisodes\) paired synthetic \mbox{5G} episodes, banking reduced bitrate by 3.8--4.5\% and rebuffering by 8.9--9.3\% versus a safety-only shield across four non-learned controllers, with mean VMAF within one point and empirical coverage \(\CPSGreedyCov\) against a \(\CPScovTarget\) target.
CPS adapts across regimes: on capacity-rich \mbox{5G} links with saturated ladders it banks bandwidth (3.8--4.5\% bitrate saved), and on capacity-limited real broadband traces---where top rungs are rarely jointly feasible and saturated---it shifts control to conformal feasibility, cutting rebuffering by 98.7\%.
\end{abstract}

\begin{keyword}
Adaptive Bitrate Streaming\sep Runtime Shielding\sep VMAF\sep Conformal Prediction\sep Certified Safety\sep Quality of Experience
\end{keyword}

\end{frontmatter}

% =========================================
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Client-side Adaptive Bitrate (ABR) policies target average Quality of Experience (QoE).
Classical controllers---Buffer-Based Adaptation (BBA)~\cite{huang2014buffer}, BOLA~\cite{spiteri2018bola}, RobustMPC~\cite{yin2015control}, bitrate-greedy selection (always requesting the top rung), and Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO)~\cite{mao2017neural,schulman2017proximal}---optimized session averages.
Those averages can obscure per-chunk risk under volatile sub-6\,GHz and mmWave \mbox{5G} throughput~\cite{narayanan2021variegated,cisco2023,seufert2015survey}.
A comfortable buffer creates the opposite problem: wasted bandwidth.
Commercial rate ladders often saturate in Video Multimethod Assessment Fusion (VMAF)~\cite{li2016vmaf}: further bitrate increases at the top often provide only marginal perceptual gains.
Requesting those rungs when the buffer is healthy consumes bandwidth that could cushion the next dip.

Recent work attacked pieces of this problem, but not the same intersection.
Conformal-ABR~\cite{zhong2026conformalabr} wrapped a throughput predictor with split-conformal intervals and selected representations under an explicit stall-risk budget.
SafeSABR~\cite{liu2025safesabr} paired risk-calibrated learning with a runtime capacity auditor on volatile broadband.
Kairos~\cite{zhong2025kairos} strengthened Model Predictive Control (MPC) with buffer-conditioned uncertainty, yet without a conformal certificate.
On the learning side, Comyco~\cite{huang2019comyco} folded VMAF into the training objective, and COREL~\cite{hu2023corel} cast ABR as a Constrained Markov Decision Process (CMDP) with a stall-time penalty during rollouts.
An expected-return or CMDP objective still leaves a per-chunk gap: guarantees hold over the sampled trajectory distribution, so an unseen blockage can induce an unsafe proposal at a single chunk boundary~\cite{hu2023corel,gu2024safereview}.
Runtime post-shields sit between the base policy and the player and can overwrite that proposal before download~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding,konighofer2025shields}.
The standard repair, however, decrements the representation index---the discrete ladder index of the proposed rung---until a buffer-feasibility test passes.
That repair is content-blind: it ignores VMAF saturation near the top of the ladder, and prior shields often used a fixed throughput scale without a stated coverage guarantee.
None of these lines combines all three ingredients that we need: a model-agnostic post-shield that overwrites an unsafe proposal, a stated conformal throughput lower bound, and VMAF-knee bandwidth banking that downshifts early on saturated upper rungs to reserve headroom.

We introduce a \emph{Certified Perceptual Shield} (CPS) that targets exactly this gap.
Until a short warm-up of recent chunks fills the calibration window, CPS uses a fixed fallback throughput scale.
It then switches to an online split-conformal lower bound with finite-sample coverage:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{VMAF-knee bandwidth banking.} Even a feasible action is lowered to the smallest rung whose VMAF lies within a small perceptual budget of the proposal. The downshift is perceptually bounded, and reclaimed bytes bank as buffer for later chunks.
  \item \textbf{Conformal throughput lower bound.} Download-time tests use an online split-conformal lower bound on throughput. Under residual exchangeability, the bound covers the next chunk with high probability at a chosen tolerated miss rate. Calibration uses recent residual ratios~\cite{vovk2005algorithmic,angelopoulos2023gentle}.
  \item \textbf{Certified feasibility.} After banking, CPS prefers a rung whose predicted download time under the throughput lower bound still fits in the remaining buffer (minus a small safety margin). Two emergency cases force the lowest rung instead: (i)~no higher rung passes that test, or (ii)~the buffer is already at or below a critical floor, so the player must request the cheapest chunk. Only when the downloaded rung passes the test and the buffer retains a minimal playout margin does this yield a per-chunk probabilistic stall certificate; it is not a promise of zero stalls.
\end{enumerate}
A pure VMAF-ranking shield is not enough on a typical ladder.
If quality never drops when the rung index rises, then among the feasible rungs the highest VMAF is always the highest index.
Allowing a small perceptual loss does not force a downshift in that case, because the top feasible rung still wins the ranking.
CPS therefore banks to the \emph{smallest} rung inside the perceptual budget, rather than maximizing VMAF among feasible rungs.
The same CPS rule wraps bitrate-greedy, BBA, and PPO hosts without changing their internal logic.

To separate banking from safety, we run three paired arms on identical episode seeds: \textsc{Raw} (unshielded), \textsc{Safety} (conformal feasibility only), and \textsc{Certified} (feasibility plus banking).
The banking effect is the \textsc{Certified} minus \textsc{Safety} contrast. The safety effect compares \textsc{Safety} or \textsc{Certified} against \textsc{Raw}.

The main contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Certified Perceptual Shield.} A model-agnostic runtime projection that combines VMAF-knee bandwidth banking, a conformal throughput lower bound with finite-sample coverage under residual exchangeability, and certified buffer feasibility.

  \item \textbf{Isolated banking evaluation.} Paired \textsc{Raw}, \textsc{Safety}, and \textsc{Certified} rollouts on greedy, BBA, BOLA, RobustMPC, and PPO hosts. We report Wilcoxon tests on bitrate and rebuffering, and Two One-Sided Tests (TOST) of VMAF within a small perceptual tolerance~\cite{lakens2017equivalence}.

  \item \textbf{Optional predictive banking (CPS-P).} This mode widens the perceptual budget dynamically from the base \(\varepsilon\) toward a risk budget \(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{risk}}\) when the buffer is low or a short look-ahead forecasts a dip.

  \item \textbf{Regime characterization.} Banking gains concentrate where ladders saturate at the top and hosts request those rungs (primary \mbox{5G} suite). Real-trace broadband stress is dominated by safety projection.

  \item \textbf{Policy--shield co-design.} An optional PPO study with CPS in the training loop. We report the QoE stall weight at which this co-trained policy crosses a shield-at-eval-only baseline---trained without CPS, then shielded only at test time.
\end{itemize}

Quantitative results appear in Section~\ref{sec:eval}. That section reports the paired \mbox{5G} and broadband campaigns, optional CPS-P, and the co-design study.

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows.
Section~\ref{sec:related} reviews related work, and Section~\ref{sec:system} formalizes the streaming model.
Section~\ref{sec:method} presents CPS and optional co-training.
Section~\ref{sec:eval} reports the evaluation, and Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes.

