\section{Related work}
\label{sec:related}

Three areas bear on client-side representation selection: model-based ABR, learned ABR, and constrained control with runtime shielding.
Our focus is the control policy and its runtime action projection; throughput prediction lies outside this scope.
Predictors such as CS2P~\cite{sun2016cs2p} played a complementary role: they fed inputs to model-based or learned controllers~\cite{yin2015control,mao2017neural}.
Beyond prediction, ABR surveys~\cite{bentaleb2018survey,seufert2015survey,peroni2025survey,timmerer2025has} and \mbox{5G/mmWave} measurements~\cite{narayanan2021variegated,fezeu2024midband} agree on one empirical lesson: control must remain robust when throughput fluctuates sharply.

\subsection{Heuristic and model-based ABR}

MPEG Dynamic Adaptive Streaming over HTTP (DASH) separates packaging from transport~\cite{sodagar2011mpeg}.
In this method, clients execute local adaptation policies based on ladder metadata and observed download conditions.
Buffer-threshold heuristics such as BBA~\cite{huang2014buffer} remained simple and interpretable.
BOLA~\cite{spiteri2018bola}, by contrast, derived its decisions from a Lyapunov-based utility formulation.
RobustMPC~\cite{yin2015control} planned ahead.
It selected representations by solving a short-horizon model predictive control problem under estimated future throughput.
The payoff is interpretable thresholds and horizons.
The cost is calibration fragility: performance degrades when hand-tuned thresholds, planning horizons, or the throughput forecasts that feed them fail to transfer across networks and devices~\cite{narayanan2021variegated,cisco2023}.
Moreover, VMAF scores often saturate on the upper rungs of a ladder, so those rungs add little perceptual quality~\cite{li2016vmaf}. Conventional feasibility rules still admit those upper rungs whenever the buffer test passes; they do not downshift early on saturated rungs to reserve throughput headroom.
Other related efforts target different stages of the delivery pipeline.
SODA~\cite{chen2024soda} pursued smoothness-aware online control. Edge assistance~\cite{mehrabi2019edge} and Oboe-style automatic tuning~\cite{akhtar2018oboe} addressed other parts of the pipeline.
Recent client-side designs also treat throughput uncertainty explicitly rather than relying on a single point forecast.
Conformal-ABR~\cite{zhong2026conformalabr} wrapped a throughput predictor with split-conformal intervals. It selected a representation under an explicit stall-risk budget. This yields distribution-free control of prediction error when the traces are exchangeable.
SafeSABR~\cite{liu2025safesabr} targeted volatile satellite broadband with risk-calibrated reinforcement learning. A runtime safety auditor then checked each policy proposal against conservative capacity bounds before download.
Kairos~\cite{zhong2025kairos} instead strengthened MPC-based ABR with streaming-aware prediction and buffer-conditioned uncertainty, improving robustness without a conformal certificate.
These lines of work address complementary failure modes: risk-aware control, runtime auditing, and robust planning.
As positioned in Section~\ref{sec:intro}, none of them combines a model-agnostic post-shield, a stated conformal throughput lower bound, and VMAF-knee banking; CPS targets that intersection at every chunk.

\subsection{Deep reinforcement learning for ABR}

Pensieve~\cite{mao2017neural} established the template: a trace-driven Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) formulation for ABR.
D-DASH~\cite{gadaleta2017ddash} and more recent PPO-based controllers~\cite{naresh2023ppoabr} followed this line.
Their neural policies combine throughput histories, buffer state, and manifest features~\cite{sutton2018reinforcement}.
Comyco~\cite{huang2019comyco} trained by Imitation Learning (IL) against a VMAF-based QoE objective and showed that perceptual signals helped inside the learned policy.
Loki~\cite{zhang2021loki} combined learned features with a rule-based model and reduced long-tail failures in production.
Its rule module limited extreme decisions, but it provided no formal feasibility certificate.
Fugu~\cite{yan2019learning} deployed learned control in live networks. It exposed the distribution shift between simulation and deployment. That shift motivates runtime safeguards beyond expected-return training.
Taken together, these systems motivate constrained learning and decision-time enforcement.
As noted in Section~\ref{sec:intro}, a Constrained Markov Decision Process (CMDP) still leaves a per-chunk enforcement gap that training alone cannot close.

\subsection{Safe/constrained Reinforcement Learning (RL), shields, and the content-blindness gap}

CMDPs and Lagrangian primal--dual methods optimize expected return under explicit constraints~\cite{altman1999constrained,paternain2019dual,achiam2017constrained}.
Safe-RL surveys catalogued related families, including shielding, Lyapunov, and risk-sensitive schemes~\cite{gu2024safereview}; Lyapunov-based safe RL~\cite{chow2018lyapunov} is a representative example.
COREL~\cite{hu2023corel} was closest to our training formulation.
It cast ABR over commercial \mbox{5G/mmWave} traces as a CMDP with a stall-time constraint.
The CMDP was solved by a primal--dual actor--critic.
That constraint, however, operated within training. It was not a standalone runtime projection that logs each intervention at decision time.
Runtime shielding takes the opposite approach: it repairs a proposed action before execution and can log each intervention~\cite{alshiekh2018shielding}.
Surveys split the family into \emph{pre-shielding}, which prunes the action set, and \emph{post-shielding}, which overwrites an unsafe proposal~\cite{konighofer2025shields}.
By that taxonomy, CPS is a post-shield.
Its safety set is drawn by a conformal feasibility test rather than a temporal-logic model.

Recent work has broadened both the learned and the uncertainty-aware branches of ABR.
NetLLM~\cite{wu2024netllm} adapted a pre-trained Large Language Model (LLM) to bitrate selection.
LingXi~\cite{jia2025lingxi} tuned ABR objectives online from user-level engagement in a large production deployment.
AAR~\cite{chen2025aar} maximized worst-case QoE under bandwidth uncertainty with an MPC controller for low-latency live streaming.
These designs improved expected or worst-case QoE, but none of them attached a per-chunk feasibility certificate to the selected representation.
On the statistical side, conformal risk control~\cite{angelopoulos2024crc} extended split-conformal guarantees from coverage to monotone losses.
Online conformal updates~\cite{angelopoulos2024online} retained long-run coverage on non-exchangeable sequences.
Conformal prediction has also become a standard tool for runtime verification and safe control of learning-enabled systems~\cite{lindemann2025cpsurvey}.
Recent shielded RL even filters actions with conformal safety margins~\cite{kwon2026adaptiveshielding}.
CPS transfers this runtime-assurance pattern to ABR and couples it with VMAF-knee banking.

By that separation, CPS pairs an optional learned performance layer with a runtime post-shield.
Learned policies such as Lagrangian PPO can pursue expected stall and smoothness budgets across training rollouts~\cite{paternain2019dual,hu2023corel}, but they cannot certify per-chunk feasibility under conformal throughput bounds.
Unlike Comyco, where VMAF enters only the learned objective~\cite{huang2019comyco}, CPS reads the ladder at decision time to bank bandwidth proactively, rather than only repairing the representation index in an emergency.
