\subsection{Streaming architecture and control loop}

We work in the standard Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) adaptive-streaming abstraction~\cite{yin2015control,mao2017neural,sodagar2011mpeg}.
A video origin or Content Delivery Network (CDN) publishes \(L\) encoded rungs.
We index them by \(\mathcal{B}=\{0,\ldots,L{-}1\}\); the DASH or HTTP Live Streaming (HLS) manifest carries the matching per-chunk sizes and perceptual metadata.
At decision step \(k\), the client fetches chunk \(k\) while holding a playout buffer \(B_k\).
Decisions advance on the chunk clock, one step per playback duration \(T_{\mathrm{c}}\)~\cite{mao2017neural,yin2015control}.
The effective trace throughput \(C^{\mathrm{tr}}_k\) is exogenous to the ABR client: the client sees only measured delivery rates, not how the base station schedules radio resources, as on mobile links where blockage and mobility abruptly change throughput~\cite{narayanan2021variegated,cisco2023}.

A base policy \(\pi\) (heuristic or learned) maps an augmented state \(s_k\), including buffer occupancy, throughput history, manifest lookahead, and the VMAF ladder (defined below), to a proposed action \(a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k\in\mathcal{B}\).
Before chunk \(k\) is downloaded, the Certified Perceptual Shield \(\mathcal{S}\) applies the projection
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:shield-map}
    \big(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k,\iota_k\big)=\mathcal{S}\!\left(s_k,a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k\right),\qquad a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k\in\mathcal{B},\ \ \iota_k\in\{0,1\},
\end{equation}
The projection returns the executed rung index \(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k\) and an intervention bit \(\iota_k\), which indicates whether the projection altered the proposal.
Only \(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k\) feeds the chunk download and buffer dynamics Equation~\eqref{eq:buffer}.
\figref{fig:arch} traces the \(a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k\!\rightarrow\!\mathcal{S}\!\rightarrow\!a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k\) path.
That path does not require a learned host: the actor, critic, and dual variables in the figure are an optional RL training branch and are unused at inference.

