Optional training-time CMDP learning enforces budgets on stall time and smoothness in expectation over rollouts~\cite{paternain2019dual,achiam2017constrained}.
It does not guarantee per-step stall avoidance from throughput uncertainty alone.
At runtime, CPS projects \(a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k\) to a feasible rung using a conformal lower bound \(\underline{C}_k\); marginal coverage \(C^{\mathrm{act}}_k\ge\underline{C}_k\) holds at rate \(1{-}\alpha\)~\cite{vovk2005algorithmic,angelopoulos2023gentle}.

\subsection{State, action, reward, and nonlinear perceptual mapping}

\textbf{State \(s_k\).}
The observation holds buffer occupancy, past throughput samples, and the previous decision.
It also includes manifest lookahead: upcoming chunk sizes across the rung ladder, plus the spatial complexity index (SI) and temporal complexity index (TI), which measure texture and motion richness~\cite{itu2023p910}.
Higher SI/TI indicate richer spatial or temporal structure in the chunk.
The VMAF ladder \(\{V_k(j)\}_{j\in\mathcal{B}}\) completes the observation, where \(V_k(j)\) is the perceptual score for chunk \(k\) at rung \(j\)~\cite{li2016vmaf}.
This last component is essential: without \(\{V_k(j)\}\), neither a learned host nor CPS can detect the content-dependent ladder saturation that bandwidth banking exploits.

\textbf{Nonlinear, content-dependent fidelity.}
Saturation arises because quality does not scale linearly with rate: for each chunk \(k\), the map \(j\mapsto V_k(j)\) is not affine in the discrete rung index \(j\).
This map also varies across chunks.
Different Group-of-Pictures (GOP) structures and texture content yield different marginal VMAF gains between adjacent rungs.
Therefore, the bitrate-to-VMAF relationship is both nonlinear and content-dependent.
Consequently, adjacent rungs may differ by a few VMAF points or by more than ten.
Quality often saturates at the upper rungs of the ladder---precisely the structure that bandwidth banking exploits.
CPS exploits any saturation that remains in a deployed ladder at decision time.
In contrast, perceptually aware Just-Noticeable Difference (JND)-based per-title encoding aims to remove this saturation at authoring time~\cite{menon2022perceptual}.

\textbf{Action.}
\(a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k\in\mathcal{B}\) is the policy's proposed rung index for chunk \(k\); the environment executes only \(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k\).

\textbf{QoE surrogate.}
Keeping to RobustMPC/Pensieve reporting conventions~\cite{yin2015control,mao2017neural}, we log a scalar per-chunk surrogate with fidelity \(q_k \equiv V_k\!\left(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k\right)\):
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:qoe-rep}
    \mathrm{QoE}^{\mathrm{(rep)}}_{k} = q_k - \mu\, \text{smooth}_k - \beta\, \tau^{\mathrm{stall}}_k,
\end{equation}
where \(\text{smooth}_k=\lvert q_k-q_{k-1}\rvert\) is the absolute VMAF change between adjacent chunks and \(\tau^{\mathrm{stall}}_k\) is the stall duration at step \(k\).
The first chunk takes a zero smoothness term.
The weights \(\mu\) and \(\beta\) are fixed in Section~\ref{sec:eval:setup}.
The stall weight \(\beta\) is inherited from Pensieve's linear QoE~\cite{mao2017neural}, where it was set for bitrate in Mbps; we keep it unchanged rather than re-tuning it for the 0--100 VMAF scale, so the logged scalar weights stalls lightly. We therefore report rebuffering separately, and the co-design analysis sweeps the stall weight explicitly (Section~\ref{sec:eval:codesign}).
Session QoE sums over chunks: \(\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_e}\mathrm{QoE}^{\mathrm{(rep)}}_{k}\).
For a learned host, optional RL training substitutes the Lagrangian CMDP objective in Section~\ref{sec:system:cmdp}~\cite{altman1999constrained,paternain2019dual}.

\subsection{Ladder saturation}
\label{sec:system:ladder}

The evaluated system reports one VMAF value per rung and video (pooled per-frame scores), so shield decisions operate on a per-title session-mean ladder that does not vary across chunks. We keep the general notation \(V_k(j)\) because the per-chunk ablation (Section~\ref{sec:eval:perchunk}) uses actual per-chunk ladders; in the headline runs \(V_k(j)\equiv V(j)\).
\tabref{tab:ladder:spacing} summarizes session-mean ladders for \(\LadNVideos\) publicly available reference titles (five Blender animations and seven natural-motion Xiph DERF clips, including Ducks Take Off).
\figref{fig:ladder} illustrates per-chunk spread on three titles with frame-level VMAF logs (Big Buck Bunny, Tears of Steel, and Sintel).
Across the twelve-title suite, \(\LadPctNonMonotone\%\) exhibit a non-monotone session-mean ladder. Mid-ladder inversions concentrate at the \(750{\to}1200\,\mathrm{kbps}\) step; the top three rungs (\(1850\), \(2850\), \(6000\,\mathrm{kbps}\)) remain nondecreasing on every title. The mean top-rung gain (6000 vs.\ 2850\,kbps) is only \(\LadTopGainMean\) VMAF points.
The session-mean top-rung gain averages across titles; saturated animation titles can appear nearly flat in \figref{fig:ladder} despite this mean.
This pattern reflects strong saturation on animation titles (e.g.\ Big Buck Bunny at \(+0.03\)) but steeper climbs on natural content (e.g.\ Park Joy at \(+14.3\)).
Two properties make banking worthwhile. First, many adjacent pairs differ by at most one VMAF point on saturated titles, so a single-step downgrade costs almost nothing perceptually. Second, the top rungs saturate frequently, allowing the knee rule in Equation~\eqref{eq:knee} to bank bytes with no material quality loss.
On the four titles with a nondecreasing session-mean ladder, max-VMAF and max-index feasible choices coincide (Proposition~\ref{prop:collapse}).
The same equivalence holds for the upper three rungs on every title; greedy banking operates mainly there.
CPS instead picks the smallest rung within the perceptual budget, preserving near-lossless downshifts under saturation.
Shield decisions use session-mean ladders, but per-chunk VMAF can invert locally (\figref{fig:ladder}); Section~\ref{sec:eval:perchunk} tests banking on these per-chunk ladders.

