\subsection{Chunk-timescale dynamics}

\subsubsection{Throughput and latency}
Let \(C^{\mathrm{tr}}_k>0\) be the trace-derived effective throughput for chunk \(k\).
Section~\ref{sec:method} defines predictor \(\hat{C}_k\) (harmonic mean of recent realizations); the conformal construction yields lower bound \(\underline{C}_k\) fed to the shield.
The realized throughput of the executed download is \(C^{\mathrm{act}}_k\); in our trace-driven simulator \(C^{\mathrm{act}}_k=C^{\mathrm{tr}}_k\).
Given \(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k\), the client downloads the manifest payload size \(\sigma_{k}(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k)\) in kilobits.
Let \(\tilde{C}_k=\max\{C^{\mathrm{act}}_k,\varepsilon_0\}\) for a small constant \(\varepsilon_0{>}0\) (the same numerical guard as in Equation~\eqref{eq:dl}), and \(d_k(j)=\sigma_{k}(j)/\tilde{C}_k\) for the nominal download duration of rung \(j\).
The executed chunk uses \(d_k\equiv d_k(a^{\mathrm{exec}}_k)\)~\cite{yin2015control}.
Feasibility timing under \(\underline{C}_k\) is defined in Section~\ref{sec:method}.

\subsubsection{Buffer and stall}
Let \(B_k\ge 0\) be the buffered playback time when the download of chunk \(k\) begins~\cite{yin2015control,mao2017neural}.
Rebuffering (stall) and the buffer update follow
\begin{equation}
    \tau^{\mathrm{stall}}_k=\max\{0,\, d_k-B_k\},
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:buffer}
    B_{k+1}=\min\bigl\{\max\{0,B_k-d_k\}+T_{\mathrm{c}},\,B_{\max}\bigr\},
\end{equation}
where \(B_{\max}\) is the player's buffer cap (\(12\,\mathrm{s}\) in our simulator unless noted).
Randomness across episodes has a single source: trace and initial-condition sampling~\cite{fcc2015mba,kvalbein2014,bentaleb2018survey}.

\subsection{Optional Lagrangian training for RL hosts}
\label{sec:system:cmdp}

When the base policy is learned, PPO training uses a Lagrangian-relaxed CMDP.
The CMDP maximizes expected fidelity under budgets on trajectory-level rebuffering and smoothness~\cite{altman1999constrained,paternain2019dual,achiam2017constrained}.
An unconstrained Markov Decision Process (MDP) optimizes only \(\mathbb{E}[\sum_k \gamma^k r_k]\). It places no explicit budget on stalls or smoothness.
Set \(c^{\mathrm{stall}}_k \equiv \tau^{\mathrm{stall}}_k\) and \(c^{\mathrm{sm}}_k \equiv \text{smooth}_k\).
The undiscounted episodic rebuffering ratio is
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{RB}(e)=\frac{1}{T^{\mathrm{play}}_{e}}\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_{e}} c^{\mathrm{stall}}_k.
\end{equation}
The mean smoothness cost is
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{SM}(e)=\frac{1}{N_{\mathrm{ch}}}\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}_{e}} c^{\mathrm{sm}}_k,
\end{equation}
with targets \(\delta_{\mathrm{rf}}{=}0.05\) (rebuffering-ratio budget) and \(\delta_{\mathrm{sm}}{=}3.5\) (mean smoothness budget in VMAF points)~\cite{yin2015control}.
Per-step normalized costs \(\widetilde{\mathrm{RB}}_k\) and \(\widetilde{\mathrm{SM}}_k\) yield the Lagrangian-relaxed PPO reward
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:rtrain-app}
    r^{\mathrm{train}}_k
    = \frac{1}{100}\Big(
        q_k
        - \lambda_r \,\widetilde{\mathrm{RB}}_k
        - \lambda_s \,\widetilde{\mathrm{SM}}_k
        - w_b\,\phi(B_k)
        - w_\ell\,\psi^{\mathrm{Lya}}_k
    \Big),
\end{equation}
where projected ascent on the empirical constraint gaps updates \((\lambda_r,\lambda_s)\)~\cite{paternain2019dual}.
The buffer-deviation term \(\phi(B_k)\) and the Lyapunov drift penalty \(\psi^{\mathrm{Lya}}_k\) are optional~\cite{neely2010stochastic}.
They steady the optimization. They do not certify per-chunk feasibility.
Finite-sample PPO with a clipped objective cannot provide that certificate~\cite{schulman2017proximal}.
CPS supplies it at deployment time.
The implementation uses Stable-Baselines~3 PPO~\cite{raffin2021sb3,schulman2017proximal}: two Tanh Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLPs) of width 256, Generalized Advantage Estimation (GAE) with discount \(\gamma{=}0.99\) and \(\lambda_{\mathrm{GAE}}{=}0.95\), dual step sizes \(\eta_r{=}0.012\) and \(\eta_s{=}0.003\), and projected ranges \(\lambda_r\in[4.3,40]\), \(\lambda_s\in[0.3,2.5]\) after a 60-episode warm-up.
The discount \(\gamma\) and the GAE parameter \(\lambda_{\mathrm{GAE}}\) are the standard PPO settings of Stable-Baselines~3 and GAE~\cite{schulman2017proximal,raffin2021sb3}.
In contrast, \(\lambda_r\) and \(\lambda_s\) are Lagrangian dual multipliers rather than fixed PPO hyperparameters; after the warm-up, projected ascent on the constraint gaps updates them within the ranges above.
These ranges follow from the VMAF scale: the lower bound of \(\lambda_r\) lies close to the stall weight used in evaluation, so the penalty is effective from the start, and the upper bound keeps the rebuffering cost commensurate with fidelity units without overwhelming the reward.
The step sizes \(\eta_r\) and \(\eta_s\) are the dual ascent rates; for constraint gaps on the order of a few hundredths, they yield gentle but stable updates, and \(\eta_s\) is smaller because the smoothness constraint acts on the per-point scale.
Both rates were stabilized by monitoring the dual traces and the achieved-versus-target constraint gap during training.
Co-design wraps the training environment in CPS, so the actor--critic observes the projected action distribution.
Shield-at-eval-only policies train without the shield.

% =========================================
\section{Method: Certified Perceptual Shield}
\label{sec:method}

CPS is a model-agnostic wrapper around any base policy that proposes \(a^{\mathrm{raw}}_k\in\mathcal{B}\).
A base policy proposes a ladder rung; CPS applies a projection before download; the player then updates the buffer under realized throughput.
The projection is deterministic: for fixed inputs it always returns the same executed rung.
What it certifies is probabilistic: feasibility is tested against a conformal throughput lower bound with a stated coverage guarantee.
The rest of this section covers the conformal estimator, the banking and feasibility projection, a predictive extension, optional PPO co-training, and the evaluation arms.

\subsection{Conformal throughput lower bound}
\label{sec:method:conformal}

The point predictor \(\hat{C}_k\) is the harmonic mean of the last \(\kappa\) observed throughputs, following the RobustMPC throughput estimator~\cite{yin2015control}.
After each step, the multiplicative residual \(r=C^{\mathrm{act}}/\hat{C}\) is appended to a rolling window of size \(W\).
With \(n\) residuals in that window, split conformal picks a conservative residual quantile at the target miss rate \(\alpha\): the entry at rank \(\lfloor\alpha(n{+}1)\rfloor\) in the sorted list (1-based indexing).
The factor \(n{+}1\) is the standard finite-sample correction; a linearly interpolated empirical quantile would under-cover on short episodes~\cite{vovk2005algorithmic,angelopoulos2023gentle}.
If that rank is undefined---or while \(n<W_{\mathrm{calib}}\) with \(W_{\mathrm{calib}}{=}20\)---we fall back to a fixed scale \(\rho_{\mathrm{fb}}{=}0.80\). That warm-up is not the finite-sample certificate.
The lower bound reads \(\underline{C}_k=\hat{C}_k\cdot q_{\alpha}\), clipped to stay at or below \(\hat{C}_k\).
Under residual exchangeability, the construction guarantees \(\mathbb{P}(C^{\mathrm{act}}\ge\underline{C}_k)\ge 1-\alpha\) once the residual window has length at least \(W_{\mathrm{calib}}\).
Given \(\underline{C}_k\), the certified download duration of rung \(j\) is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:dl}
    d_k(j)=\min\!\left\{\frac{\sigma_k(j)}{\underline{C}_k+\varepsilon_0},\ 60\right\}.
\end{equation}
Both \(\sigma_k(j)\) and \(\underline{C}_k\) use kilobit units (\(\mathrm{kb}\) and \(\mathrm{kb/s}\)), so their ratio is already in seconds; \(\varepsilon_0{>}0\) and the outer \(\min\{\cdot,60\}\) are numerical guards against near-zero denominators.

\begin{proposition}[\textbf{Per-chunk stall certificate}]
\label{prop:feas}
Assume chunk \(k\), executed index \(j\), and conformal lower bound \(\underline{C}_k\) computed with miscoverage \(\alpha\).
Suppose the following three conditions hold:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item[(i)] \(B_k\ge 0.1\,\mathrm{s}\) (buffer floor);
  \item[(ii)] \(\bar{d}_k(j)\le\max\{B_k-m,0.1\}\), where \(\bar{d}_k(j)=\sigma_k(j)/\underline{C}_k\) is the unguarded certified duration;
  \item[(iii)] \(C^{\mathrm{act}}_k\ge\underline{C}_k\).
\end{enumerate}
Then \(\tau^{\mathrm{stall}}_k=0\).
The shield (Section~\ref{sec:method:shield}) tests the guarded duration \(d_k(j)\) of Equation~\eqref{eq:dl}, which equals \(\bar{d}_k(j)\cdot\underline{C}_k/(\underline{C}_k+\varepsilon_0)\) whenever the \(60\,\mathrm{s}\) cap is inactive; with \(\varepsilon_0\ll\underline{C}_k\) the two tests coincide up to this negligible factor, and the proposition applies to chunks that pass (ii).
Condition~(i) is required so that \(\max\{B_k-m,0.1\}\le B_k\).
Separately, under residual exchangeability, \(\mathbb{P}(C^{\mathrm{act}}_k\ge\underline{C}_k)\ge 1-\alpha\) at each step where the finite-sample bound is defined (\(n\ge W_{\mathrm{calib}}\)).
That coverage statement concerns the throughput lower bound alone; it is not itself a stall claim.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By (iii) and \(\underline{C}_k>0\), \(\tilde{C}_k\ge C^{\mathrm{act}}_k\ge\underline{C}_k\), so the realized download time satisfies \(\sigma_k(j)/\tilde{C}_k\le\bar{d}_k(j)\). By (ii) and (i), \(\bar{d}_k(j)\le\max\{B_k-m,0.1\}\le B_k\); hence \(\tau^{\mathrm{stall}}_k=\max\{0,d_k-B_k\}=0\).
Coverage follows from standard split-conformal prediction with the \((n{+}1)\) rank correction~\cite{vovk2005algorithmic,angelopoulos2023gentle}.
\end{proof}

\emph{Scope of the guarantee.}
Two parts of the certificate behave differently.
The feasibility guarantee is unconditional only when the executed rung satisfies \(\bar{d}_k(j)\le\max\{B_k-m,0.1\}\) and \(B_k\ge 0.1\,\mathrm{s}\).
If the realized throughput then meets or exceeds \(\underline{C}_k\), that chunk cannot stall.
The shield can still select rung \(0\) when \(B_k\le B_{\mathrm{crit}}\), or when the decrement loop reaches \(0\) while \(d_k(0)\) fails the test (Section~\ref{sec:method:shield}).
Those chunks are outside the certificate.

Only the throughput bound is probabilistic.
It provides marginal coverage over the exchangeable residual window, \(\mathbb{P}(C^{\mathrm{act}}_k\ge\underline{C}_k)\ge 1-\alpha\), averaged over residual draws rather than per chunk, and only after warm-up.
It does not provide coverage conditional on the current buffer or throughput regime.
Distribution-free split conformal cannot do so without stronger assumptions~\cite{angelopoulos2023gentle}.
CPS therefore certifies feasibility on every chunk whose bound holds and whose rung passed the test---under exchangeability the bound fails with probability at most \(\alpha\); it does not promise a stall-free session.

Abrupt \mbox{5G/mmWave} Line-of-Sight/Non-Line-of-Sight (LOS/NLOS) transitions and scheduling shifts can break residual exchangeability~\cite{narayanan2021variegated}. Our online defense is a sliding residual window of length \(W\). The recent ratios play two roles: they calibrate \(q_{\alpha}\), and they are the ratios immediately preceding the chunk we predict. The bound therefore tracks local nonstationarity, even though finite-sample exactness holds only on an exchangeable window. If drift is large, coverage decays. The decay is bounded by a total-variation term between calibration and test residuals~\cite{barber2023beyond}. A weighted-conformal variant that down-weights stale residuals is a natural extension, which we leave to future work.
Empirically, we treat reported coverage as the operational check of Proposition~\ref{prop:feas}.
The headline rate averages over every chunk with a defined bound, including the \(W_{\mathrm{calib}}\) warm-up steps that use \(\rho_{\mathrm{fb}}\).
On the primary \mbox{5G} pool, that inclusive rate is \(\CPSGreedyCov\) against the target \(\CPScovTarget\).
Restricting to post--warm-up chunks alone raises coverage to \(\CPSGreedyCovPost\) (warm-up chunks alone: \(\CPSGreedyCovWarm\)); Section~\ref{sec:eval} reports both views.
Real broadband traces (Section~\ref{sec:eval:improved}) and tighter \(\alpha\) (Section~\ref{sec:eval:sensitivity}) can sit marginally below the target.
This is what the theory predicts: short calibration windows and nonstationary traces both erode finite-sample coverage.

\subsection{Banking and certified feasibility}
\label{sec:method:shield}

\emph{Safety set.} Indices that never exceed the proposal and fit the buffer margin \(m\) form
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:asafe}
    \mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe},k}(a)=\bigl\{j\le a:\ d_k(j)\le \max\{B_k-m,0.1\}\bigr\}.
\end{equation}
\emph{VMAF-knee banking.} The perceptual budget \(\varepsilon\) is a tolerance in VMAF points. Even when \(a\) is feasible, CPS steps down to the smallest rung whose VMAF lies within \(\varepsilon\) of the proposal, namely
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:knee}
    j^{\mathrm{knee}}_k(a)=\min\bigl\{j\le a:\ V_k(a)-V_k(j)\le\varepsilon\bigr\},
\end{equation}
The shield then projects \(j^{\mathrm{knee}}_k(a)\) onto \(\mathcal{A}_{\mathrm{safe},k}(a)\); safety always takes precedence over \(\varepsilon\).

\emph{Remark.}
Banking may run a lower rung than the proposal, and a lower rung can only shorten \(d_k(\cdot)\).
Once Equation~\eqref{eq:asafe} holds, safety projection therefore keeps the certificate intact.

Algorithm~\ref{alg:cps} states the CPS projection that realizes Equations~\eqref{eq:asafe}--\eqref{eq:knee}.
If \(B_k\le B_{\mathrm{crit}}\), CPS selects the lowest rung with no feasibility test.
Otherwise it computes the knee index from Equation~\eqref{eq:knee} under the effective budget \(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{eff}}\).
This budget equals \(\varepsilon\) unless predictive mode is on (Section~\ref{sec:method:predictive}).
CPS then decrements the index until the certified duration in Equation~\eqref{eq:dl} respects the margin \(m\), or until the index is \(0\).
If rung \(0\) still fails the test, the procedure still selects it so that playback can continue: it is an emergency best-effort choice without the stall certificate of Proposition~\ref{prop:feas}.
The same holds for the \(B_{\mathrm{crit}}\) path.
The shield never upgrades a proposal.
Diagnostics log banked bits, VMAF surrendered, and whether realized throughput met \(\underline{C}_k\) (empirical coverage).

\begin{algorithm}[!t]
\footnotesize
\caption{Certified Perceptual Shield (compact)}
\label{alg:cps}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\STATE \textbf{Input:} \(a^{\mathrm{raw}}\), \(B_k\), \(\{\sigma_k(j),V_k(j)\}\), conformal estimator; parameters \((\varepsilon,m,B_{\mathrm{crit}})\)
\STATE \textbf{Output:} \((a^{\mathrm{exec}},\iota)\) with \(\iota=\mathbf{1}[a^{\mathrm{exec}}\neq a^{\mathrm{raw}}]\)
\STATE \(a\leftarrow\mathrm{clip}(a^{\mathrm{raw}})\); \(\underline{C}_k\leftarrow\) conformal lower bound; define \(d_k(\cdot)\) via Equation~\eqref{eq:dl}
\IF{\(B_k\le B_{\mathrm{crit}}\)} \STATE \textbf{return} \((0,\mathbf{1}[a\neq0])\) \ENDIF
\STATE compute \(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{eff}}\) (Section~\ref{sec:method:predictive}); \(j\leftarrow j^{\mathrm{knee}}_k(a)\) via Equation~\eqref{eq:knee} with \(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{eff}}\)
\WHILE{\(j>0\) and \(d_k(j)>\max\{B_k-m,0.1\}\)} \STATE \(j\leftarrow j-1\) \ENDWHILE
\STATE \(a^{\mathrm{exec}}\leftarrow\min\{a,j\}\); \textbf{return} \((a^{\mathrm{exec}},\mathbf{1}[a^{\mathrm{exec}}\neq a])\)
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

Banking distinguishes CPS from prior VMAF-ranking shields, and the distinction is substantive. On any nondecreasing prefix of a ladder, a ranking shield provably banks nothing.

\begin{proposition}[\textbf{Ranking shields collapse on monotone ladders}]
\label{prop:collapse}
Assume chunk \(k\) and suppose the VMAF ladder is nondecreasing in the rung index, \(V_k(0)\le\cdots\le V_k(L-1)\), and that download time \(d_k(\cdot)\) is nondecreasing in the rung index.
Let \(\mathcal{F}=\{j: d_k(j)\le \max\{B_k-m,0.1\}\}\) be the feasible set, as in Equation~\eqref{eq:asafe}.
Then any shield that selects \(\arg\max_{j\in\mathcal{F}} V_k(j)\) selects the highest feasible index in \(\mathcal{F}\), independently of any perceptual-loss budget \(\varepsilon\).
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Because \(d_k\) is nondecreasing, \(\mathcal{F}\) is a contiguous prefix of the ladder ending at its largest index.
Because \(V_k\) is also nondecreasing, the feasible rung with highest VMAF is exactly that largest index; the budget \(\varepsilon\) never changes the choice.
\end{proof}
Ranking by retained VMAF is therefore equivalent to selecting the highest feasible index, and it frees no bandwidth.
CPS instead uses the proposal's quality and steps down to the smallest rung within \(\varepsilon\) of it, as stated in Equation~\eqref{eq:knee}.
This mechanism lets CPS exploit rate--quality saturation: when top rungs carry near-identical VMAF, the knee lies well below the highest feasible index, and the freed bytes bank as buffer.

The evaluation isolates banking from safety with three paired arms (\textsc{Raw}, \textsc{Safety}, \textsc{Certified}); Section~\ref{sec:eval:setup} defines them relative to Algorithm~\ref{alg:cps}.

\subsection{Predictive and risk-aware perceptual budget}
\label{sec:method:predictive}

A fixed perceptual budget spends the same tolerance on every chunk.
That spend is unnecessary while the buffer is comfortable, and it can be too small when a dip is imminent.
CPS-P, the predictive mode introduced above, exists to separate those two cases: it keeps the base budget \(\varepsilon\) in the comfortable regime and widens it only under the risk triggers below.
Section~\ref{sec:eval:improved} compares this mode with a larger static budget.

The predictive budget widens when slack is scarce: it banks lightly when buffer headroom is ample and more aggressively when risk arises.
While the buffer stays comfortable, \(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{eff}}{=}\varepsilon\) holds quality steady.
Two risk triggers widen the budget: (i)~the buffer \(B_k\) falls below a risk threshold \(B_{\mathrm{risk}}\); (ii)~a short \(H\)-step look-ahead under a planning throughput floor (a low quantile of recent observations) forecasts a dip below \(B_{\mathrm{risk}}\).
In either case, the effective budget is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:eps-eff}
    \varepsilon_{\mathrm{eff}}=\varepsilon+(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{risk}}-\varepsilon)\cdot f(B_k,\mathrm{lookahead}),
\end{equation}
where \(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{risk}}>\varepsilon\) is the wider risk budget.
Let \(\underline{C}_k^{\mathrm{plan}}\) be the planning throughput: the conformal lower bound, optionally floored by a low quantile of recent observations when dip forecasting is enabled.
Simulate \(H\) future downloads of the proposed rung under \(\underline{C}_k^{\mathrm{plan}}\), write \(\hat{B}_{k+h}\) for the resulting buffer after step \(h\), and let \(\hat{B}^{\min}_k=\min_{1\le h\le H}\hat{B}_{k+h}\).
Then
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:f-ramp}
    f(B_k,\mathrm{lookahead})
    =
    \begin{cases}
      1, & \hat{B}^{\min}_k<B_{\mathrm{risk}},\\[0.3em]
      \mathrm{clip}\!\Bigl(\dfrac{B_{\mathrm{risk}}-B_k}{B_{\mathrm{risk}}},0,1\Bigr), & \text{otherwise,}
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
so \(f\) rises from \(0\) at a comfortable buffer to \(1\) at or below \(B_{\mathrm{risk}}\), and jumps to \(1\) if the look-ahead predicts a breach.
The shield can therefore pre-bank more deeply, before a stall forces an emergency cut.
CPS-P denotes the same CPS projection with this risk-aware \(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{eff}}\) in place of a fixed \(\varepsilon\); the conformal bound and feasibility test are unchanged.
Operating values for \(\varepsilon\), \(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{risk}}\), \(B_{\mathrm{risk}}\), and \(H\) appear in Section~\ref{sec:eval:setup}.

\subsection{Optional learned hosts and co-design}
\label{sec:method:codesign}

CPS wraps any host and does not require a learned policy; heuristic and model-based controllers use the same projection.
When we optionally study a learned host, we train a standard PPO policy (optionally Lagrangian-shaped) with the Stable-Baselines~3 library~\cite{raffin2021sb3,schulman2017proximal} on content-aware observations: throughput history, buffer, spatial and temporal complexity (SI/TI), and the VMAF ladder.
We do not propose a new RL algorithm; PPO is one optional host among others.
The subsections above define a host-agnostic shield.
This subsection asks a separate question: whether training inside that shield changes the policy the shield later wraps.
By \emph{co-design} we mean combining learning with the shield during training: wrapping the training environment in CPS changes the action distribution observed by the actor--critic, so the policy can learn to propose bolder rungs that banking later trims for safety.
To gauge the effect, we compare two policies under identical \mbox{5G} train/test provenance: one trained without the shield (shield-at-eval-only), one co-trained with CPS. Both face CPS-P at test time.

\subsection{Implementation notes}
\label{sec:method:impl}

Shield and predictive-banking defaults for the primary experiments appear in Section~\ref{sec:eval:setup} (\tabref{tab:cps:hypers}).
For the first \(W_{\mathrm{calib}}\) chunks of every episode, before the residual window supports a finite-sample quantile, the fallback scale is set to \(\rho_{\mathrm{fb}}\); that warm-up lies outside the certificate.
Each episode has \(48\) chunks and the residual window resets at the episode boundary, so a long nominal window \(W\) need not fill within one episode.
Empirical coverage is the fraction of steps with \(C^{\mathrm{act}}\ge\underline{C}_k\), counted over all chunks including warm-up.
Per decision, the projection runs in \(O(L{+}H)\) over the \(L\) ladder rungs and an \(H\)-step look-ahead. Conformal-quantile upkeep on the length-\(W\) residual window costs \(O(\log W)\) per update with an ordered container (insertion, eviction, and rank lookup).
Across \(20{,}000\) rollout decisions on a commodity CPU (\(L{=}6\), full predictive shield), the per-decision wall-clock elapsed time is \(131\,\mu\mathrm{s}\) at the median and \(310\,\mu\mathrm{s}\) at the \(99\)th percentile. This is roughly \(10^{-5}\) of a \(4\,\mathrm{s}\) chunk budget. CPS therefore runs inline, and the control loop is unaffected.

% =========================================
\section{Experimental evaluation}
\label{sec:eval}

We organize the evaluation around four research questions:

\begin{itemize}
  \item[\textbf{RQ1}] \emph{Banking vs.\ safety.} Does VMAF-knee banking reduce bitrate and rebuffering relative to conformal safety-only projection, with VMAF inside \(\pm\varepsilon\)?
  \item[\textbf{RQ2}] \emph{Coverage.} Does empirical conformal coverage meet the finite-sample target \(1{-}\alpha\)?
  \item[\textbf{RQ3}] \emph{Budget extensions.} Relative to the default budget \(\varepsilon\), do CPS-P or a larger static \(\varepsilon\) change bitrate and rebuffering savings?
  \item[\textbf{RQ4}] \emph{Robustness.} Does the banking stage (banked bitrate and realized per-chunk perceptual cost) remain effective on non-monotone per-chunk ladders? Do the RQ1--RQ2 results remain stable as \(\varepsilon\) and \(\alpha\) vary?
\end{itemize}
A single-seed co-design study appears in Section~\ref{sec:eval:codesign}.

\subsection{Experimental setup and datasets}
\label{sec:eval:setup}

The testbed is a multi-video, chunk-timescale simulator with \(N_{\mathrm{ch}}{=}48\) chunks per episode, \(T_{\mathrm{c}}{=}4\,\mathrm{s}\), and buffer cap \(B_{\max}{=}12\,\mathrm{s}\) unless noted. It replays a throughput trace chunk by chunk: for each chunk it applies the executed rung's size, computes the download time and stall from the trace throughput, and updates the buffer with Equation~\eqref{eq:buffer}. It drives twelve publicly available reference titles with session-mean VMAF ladders~\cite{li2016vmaf}: five Blender open animations~\cite{blenderfilms}---Big Buck Bunny, Tears of Steel, Sintel, Elephants Dream, and Cosmos Laundromat---and seven natural-motion Xiph DERF clips---Park Joy, Old Town Cross, Rush Hour, Into Tree, Sunflower, Kristen \& Sara, and Ducks Take Off~\cite{xiphderf}.
We select them for ladder structure, not to cover many content genres: saturation-rich animation such as Big Buck Bunny and Tears of Steel, steep natural motion such as Park Joy and Ducks Take Off, wide-span DERF clips such as Into Tree and Old Town Cross, and mixed held-out titles such as Sintel, Cosmos Laundromat, and Kristen \& Sara for generalization checks.
Nine titles appear in PPO co-design training; three---Sintel, Cosmos Laundromat, and Kristen \& Sara---are never seen during PPO training, to test generalization to unseen titles.
Because CPS acts only on the observed VMAF ladder and buffer state, ladder shape---not semantic content---drives its effect. The twelve-title suite therefore spans both saturated and steep/inverted session-mean ladders, with \(\LadPctNonMonotone\%\) non-monotone.

\textbf{Primary \mbox{5G} suite.} Public per-chunk \mbox{5G/mmWave} logs suitable for chunk-level ABR replay remain scarce~\cite{narayanan2021variegated,fezeu2024midband}. We therefore built a synthetic \mbox{5G} trace generator; its test traces, held out from the co-design training seeds, form the primary pool of \(\CPSepisodes\) paired episodes. The generator is configured as follows.
Each trace lasts \(1000\,\mathrm{s}\) at a baseline of \(9000\,\mathrm{kbps}\). Every \(40\,\mathrm{s}\), starting at offset \(20+(i \bmod M)\), a \(4\,\mathrm{s}\) interval is replaced by a dip drawn uniformly from \([500,1200]\,\mathrm{kbps}\) on the test set and from \([400,1300]\,\mathrm{kbps}\) on the training set. The trace is then scaled by an independent factor from \(\mathrm{U}(0.85,1.15)\) and clipped to \([80,50000]\,\mathrm{kbps}\).
The test set contains \(50\) traces (seed \(42\), \(M{=}17\)). The training set contains \(80\) traces (seed \(7000\), \(M{=}23\)); here \(i\) indexes the dip and \(M\) is a trace-generator offset period, unrelated to the buffer margin \(m\).
The broadband suite below provides a real-trace counterpart.
Train and test trace pools come from the same generator with different seeds and dip ranges, so they are seed-disjoint rather than source-disjoint. Co-design policies train on the training pool alone and face held-out test seeds.

\textbf{Broadband suite.} The broadband suite uses real-measured traces: the Federal Communications Commission (FCC) Measuring Broadband America (MBA) fixed-line logs~\cite{fcc2015mba} and the Norway/Nornet mobile-broadband mobility logs~\cite{kvalbein2014}, with 27 held-out test traces that are source-disjoint from training. Both are widely used public datasets in ABR research. They provide a real-network counterpart to the primary \mbox{5G} suite and show where banking offers limited headroom.

\textbf{Hosts.} Five host families cover the ABR design space: bitrate-greedy, always at the top rung; BBA; BOLA, the Lyapunov buffer-based rule of the dash.js reference player~\cite{spiteri2018bola,spiteri2018dashjs}; robust model-predictive control via RobustMPC~\cite{yin2015control}; and two PPO policies: a content-aware policy that also observes SI/TI and the VMAF ladder (Section~\ref{sec:method:codesign}), and a content-blind Pensieve-style policy without these content features~\cite{mao2017neural}. RobustMPC is a five-segment planner over a throughput prediction discounted by recent worst-case error. CPS carries no host bias: one wrapper, applied at inference across all five families, tuned to none of them.

\textbf{Evaluation configurations and paired design.} We call each shield configuration an \emph{arm}. Every host rolls out \textsc{Raw}, \textsc{Safety}, and \textsc{Certified} on identical Python seeds---the same video--trace pairs.
\textsc{Raw} runs \(a^{\mathrm{raw}}\) unshielded.
\textsc{Safety} runs Algorithm~\ref{alg:cps} with banking switched off by setting \(j^{\mathrm{knee}}{=}a\), so only conformal feasibility remains.
\textsc{Certified} runs the full CPS (banking plus feasibility).
Pairing fixes the random seed and the \((\text{video},\text{trace})\) assignment across arms, so metrics differ only because of shielding, not because of resampling.
In paired evaluations every method observes the same trace \(C^{\mathrm{tr}}_k\).
Each paired contrast is the per-episode metric difference on the same \((\text{video},\text{trace})\) seed across arms; for example, the \textsc{Certified}-arm value minus the \textsc{Safety}-arm value on the same episode.

\textbf{Metrics and statistical tests.} We report mean delivered bitrate, mean rebuffering per episode, stall-free rate, mean VMAF, intervention rate, and empirical conformal coverage. Significance rests on paired Wilcoxon signed-rank tests with zero-method \texttt{zsplit} and on TOST for equivalence of VMAF within \(\pm\varepsilon{=}\CPSepsilon\)~\cite{lakens2017equivalence}.
\tabref{tab:cps:hypers} lists the primary-\mbox{5G} defaults, including \(\alpha{=}\CPSalpha\) with coverage target \(\CPScovTarget\), \(\varepsilon{=}\CPSepsilon\), warm-up \(W_{\mathrm{calib}}{=}20\) with fallback \(\rho_{\mathrm{fb}}{=}0.80\), and the CPS-P risk settings.
We set \(\varepsilon{=}\CPSepsilon\) as both the knee budget and the TOST margin.

