\subsection{Predictive banking and broadband boundary (RQ3)}
\label{sec:eval:improved}

With the risk-aware budget \(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{eff}}\) of Equation~\eqref{eq:eps-eff} and dip forecasting enabled under CPS-P, which starts from the default base budget \(\varepsilon{=}\CPSepsilon\) and widens it up to \(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{risk}}{=}4\) VMAF points, certified banking vs.\ safety reaches a 14.6\% bitrate reduction for greedy and 12.5\% for BBA, and cuts rebuffering by 12.6\% and 13.0\%, respectively, at mean VMAF changes of \(\CPSGreedyImpVMcs\) and \(\CPSBbaImpVMcs\). Both contrasts are against the same \textsc{Safety} arm; with a fixed \(\varepsilon{=}\CPSepsilon\), the corresponding bitrate savings are 4.5\% and 4.1\% (\tabref{tab:cps:full}).
A static budget \(\varepsilon{=}2\) on the same greedy host saves 14.4\% bitrate and 30.8\% rebuffering at a mean VMAF change of \(-0.17\) (\tabref{tab:abl:eps}), compared with 14.6\%, 12.6\%, and \(\CPSGreedyImpVMcs\) under CPS-P.
At a nearly identical bitrate saving, static \(\varepsilon{=}2\) therefore achieves a larger rebuffering reduction at a slightly smaller mean VMAF cost than CPS-P in this evaluation.
CPS-P differs in how it spends the budget: it keeps \(\varepsilon{=}\CPSepsilon\) while the buffer is comfortable and widens toward \(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{risk}}\) only under forecast risk. We did not measure quality separately in stable periods, so the present data do not establish an advantage of CPS-P over a well-chosen static budget.

The broadband suite marks the edge of these gains. On source-disjoint real-measured broadband traces from FCC MBA and Norway/Nornet~\cite{fcc2015mba,kvalbein2014}, spanning 27 held-out traces and \(\CPSepisodes\) paired video--trace episodes, greedy certified--safety bitrate savings shrink to 0.9\%; see \tabref{tab:cps:bb}. Top rungs are seldom jointly feasible and saturated, so banking has little remaining headroom.
Even so, safety vs.\ raw still reduces rebuffering by 98.7\%. Raw greedy already stalls for most of each broadband session, as in \tabref{tab:cps:bb}.
Because capacity limits keep saturated top rungs out of the feasible set, the conformal feasibility layer carries the main control burden.
On these 27 held-out traces at \(\alpha{=}\CPSalpha\), empirical conformal coverage is \(\CPSbbCov\), near the \(\CPScovTarget\) target. The primary \mbox{5G} pool, over \(\CPSepisodes\) episodes, reaches \(\CPSGreedyCov\); see \tabref{tab:cps:full} and \figref{fig:cps:coverage}.
The ``BB greedy'' group in \figref{fig:cps:overview} makes the regime split explicit: banking savings are largest on the primary \mbox{5G} suite.
This split follows from the mechanism: CPS banking pays off precisely when the ladder knee sits within reach, and broadband places it out of reach.

