\paragraph{Coverage under injected drift}
To stress-test exchangeability without new traces, we replay the primary \mbox{5G} pool with a mid-episode throughput shock on chunks \(28\)--\(37\): multiply trace throughput by \(0.3\), mimicking a sudden LOS loss or an NLOS dip.
We report two related views, neither of which should be confused with the inclusive headline \(\CPSGreedyCov\).
First, phase-mean coverage over valid chunks after a \(20\)-chunk conformal warm-up is \(0.928\) pre-shock with \(n{=}1{,}632\), \(0.929\) during the shock with \(n{=}2{,}040\), and \(0.944\) post-shock with \(n{=}2{,}040\), all against the \(0.90\) target on the greedy certified arm over \(\CPSepisodes\) episodes.
Those phase averages therefore stay at or above the target through the shock.
Second, \figref{fig:cps:coverage_drift} plots episode-averaged \emph{rolling} coverage with window \(W{=}20\) chunks under the same shock protocol.
That rolling curve starts near \(0.69\) in the grey warm-up band because each window still mixes fallback residuals; it is a different aggregation from the inclusive per-chunk mean \(\CPSGreedyCov\) and from the post--warm-up rate \(\CPSGreedyCovPost\) on the unshocked primary pool.
The sliding residual window then keeps phase-mean coverage on target after calibration, consistent with Section~\ref{sec:method:conformal}.

