\subsection{Learned hosts and co-design}
\label{sec:eval:codesign}

We report two PPO settings separately. The primary host is a content-aware policy trained on source-disjoint broadband traces and evaluated, without further training, on \mbox{5G}---the model-agnostic deployment case (\tabref{tab:cps:ppo}). The co-design pair comprises two policies trained on matched \mbox{5G} traces---one shield-at-eval-only, one co-trained with CPS in the loop---compared on their certified arms (\tabref{tab:cps:codesign} and \figref{fig:cps:codesign}).
On the primary broadband-trained policy, certified banking vs.\ safety saves 35.3\% bitrate and reduces rebuffering by 79.3\%, with \(p{=}\CPSPropPRebCs\), at VMAF \(\Delta{=}\CPSPropVMcs\) and TOST within \(\pm\varepsilon\).
As shown in \tabref{tab:cps:ppo}, the bitrate cut exceeds that of greedy or BBA because the \textsc{Raw} mean bitrate is \(1589\,\mathrm{kb/s}\), not the top rung. Safety already lowers it to \(1278\,\mathrm{kb/s}\). Certified banking then lowers it to \(827\,\mathrm{kb/s}\).
The \(35.3\%\) saving is thus measured from this mid-ladder \textsc{Safety} operating point, so banking is not confined to saturated upper rungs.
On more conservative hosts such as PPO it also exploits mid-ladder flats and inversions (Section~\ref{sec:system:ladder}) to cut rebuffering by \(79.3\%\) inside the perceptual budget.
Under CPS-P, banking saves 21.9\% bitrate and reduces rebuffering by 29.6\%, mirroring the predictive-banking gains on non-learned hosts in Section~\ref{sec:eval:improved}.

