\emph{Co-design.} Both policies are trained on matched \mbox{5G} traces with a seed-disjoint test. Under CPS-P at eval, the co-trained policy's certified arm attains VMAF \(\CPScoCpsVM\) and bitrate \(\CPScoCpsBr\)\,kb/s, against \(\CPScoRegVM\)/\(\CPScoRegBr\) for shield-at-eval-only operation.
The \(\Delta\) row in \tabref{tab:cps:codesign} is the difference of those certified-arm means, \(+\CPScoDVM\) VMAF and \(+\CPScoDReb\)\,s rebuffering, and it matches the rows above: co-trained minus shield-at-eval-only VMAF, \(\CPScoCpsVM-\CPScoRegVM\), and rebuffering, \(\CPScoCpsReb-\CPScoRegReb\)\,s.
The fidelity gain can be weighed against its stall cost through \(\mathrm{QoE}(w)=\overline{\mathrm{VMAF}}-w\cdot\mathrm{rebuffer(s)}\). The mean-QoE crossover is \(w^{\star}{=}\CPScoWstar\) VMAF points per second, the ratio of the \(\Delta\) row entries: co-design favors fidelity-oriented weights \(w<w^{\star}\); shield-at-eval-only prevails where stall aversion is stronger.
Intuitively, \(w^{\star}\) is the stall penalty at which the co-trained fidelity gain and extra rebuffering exactly balance in mean QoE; weights above \(w^{\star}\) favor the shield-at-eval-only policy.
Both reported \(p\)-values equal \(\CPScoPVM\); that value is \(1/20{,}000\), the floor of the sign-flip test, not a Wilcoxon tail probability. The bootstrap Confidence Interval (CI) \([\CPScoDVMlo,\CPScoDVMhi]\) is the magnitude statement, with stall cost \([\CPScoDRebLo,\CPScoDRebHi]\)\,s and crossover \([\CPScoWstarLo,\CPScoWstarHi]\); the median crossover is \(\CPScoWstarMed\), and \(\Delta\)VMAF is positive in \(\CPScoDVMposPct\%\) of resamples.
Co-design uses a single training seed per policy; see Section~\ref{sec:limits}.

