\subsection{Per-chunk (non-monotone) ladder ablation (RQ4)}
\label{sec:eval:perchunk}

The headline runs apply the knee rule to each title's pooled session-mean ladder in \tabref{tab:ladder:spacing}: four of twelve ladders are fully nondecreasing, while the other eight are non-monotone only at mid-ladder from \(750\) to \(1200\,\mathrm{kbps}\). The top three rungs remain nondecreasing on every title. We therefore test whether banking remains effective on the actual per-chunk ladders, which are often non-monotone: \(\PCinvPct\%\) of the \(\PCnChunks\) chunks hold at least one inverted adjacent pair; see \figref{fig:ladder}.
The per-chunk ablation uses frame-level VMAF logs on Big Buck Bunny, Tears of Steel, and Sintel, the same three titles as \figref{fig:ladder}. The ablation reads \(\PCnChunks\) chunks from the aggregated per-chunk export; the figure aggregates \(\LadNChunks\) chunks from raw per-frame JSON, so the two counts differ.
To test this, we replay the banking stage on the per-chunk logs. We compare a shield that sees only the pooled ladder with one that sees the per-chunk ladder; see \tabref{tab:perchunk} and \figref{fig:cps:perchunk}. Banking alone depends on VMAF; the feasibility projection ignores it and stays identical in both cases, which isolates the effect of the pooling simplification.

Three findings hold at the default \(\varepsilon{=}\PCeps\), spanning correctness, savings, and pooling error.
First, the knee rule remains well defined on inverted ladders and banks more, not less: mean saved bitrate is \(\PCbwPerchunk\%\) per-chunk against \(\PCbwPooled\%\) pooled, because a lower rung that inverts above the proposal satisfies the budget immediately---inversions widen banking opportunity rather than breaking the rule.
Second, a per-chunk shield keeps realized per-chunk perceptual cost within budget by construction, with mean \(\PCcostPerchunk\) VMAF points and never above \(\varepsilon\), so the ablation validates chunk-wise enforcement when true ladders are available.
Third, pooling is not free. Judged against the actual per-chunk VMAF, the pooled-ladder shield overruns the budget on \(\PCviolPooled\%\) of chunks, with mean cost \(\PCcostPooled\) points, which explains why session-mean evaluation can understate perceptual spend even when banking bytes look similar.


Taken together, the pooled-ladder headline numbers are, if anything, conservative with respect to banking savings.
Per-chunk operation is sound: it eliminates residual budget violations at the cost of a per-chunk VMAF feed, and it aligns the content-diversity motivation with the pooled evaluation.

