\subsection{Sensitivity to \texorpdfstring{\(\varepsilon\)}{epsilon} and \texorpdfstring{\(\alpha\)}{alpha} (RQ4)}
\label{sec:eval:sensitivity}

The headline runs fix the pair at \(\varepsilon{=}\CPSepsilon\) and \(\alpha{=}\CPSalpha\). \tabref{tab:abl:eps}, \tabref{tab:abl:alpha}, and \figref{fig:cps:ablation} vary one parameter at a time on the greedy host and the primary \mbox{5G} pool of \(\CPSepisodes\) paired episodes over the twelve-title roster. The default cell with \(\varepsilon{=}\CPSepsilon\) and \(\alpha{=}\CPSalpha\) is the same evaluation as \tabref{tab:cps:full}; the other cells are a one-parameter sensitivity sweep under the same seeding protocol.
We first consider the perceptual budget. Sweeping \(\varepsilon\in\{\AblEpsLo,\dots,\AblEpsHi\}\) at \(\alpha{=}\CPSalpha\) trades banking for fidelity.
The bitrate saved relative to the \textsc{Safety} arm rises from 2.0\% to 24.6\%, while the mean VMAF change remains negligible---at most \(\AblVmMaxAbs\) points---and never leaves the matching \(\pm\varepsilon\) envelope. Wider budgets therefore trade fidelity slack for banking headroom in a predictable way.
Empirical conformal coverage remains invariant with \(\varepsilon\). Coverage depends on the throughput trace and calibrator alone, not the banking budget, which shows that banking and the feasibility certificate operate independently.
We next consider miscoverage. Sweeping \(\alpha\in\{0.05,0.10,0.20\}\) at \(\varepsilon{=}\CPSepsilon\) on the same primary \mbox{5G} pool moves empirical coverage in step with the finite-sample target \(1{-}\alpha\): coverage is \(\AblCovMax\), \(0.919\), and \(\AblCovMin\) for targets \(0.95\), \(0.90\) (default), and \(0.80\), respectively.
Tightening \(\alpha\) raises both coverage and the intervention rate; loosening it lowers both, as the \((n{+}1)\) construction predicts.
At \(\alpha{=}0.05\) the mean is \(0.945\), marginally below the \(0.95\) target over \(\CPSepisodes\) paired episodes; Section~\ref{sec:method:conformal} explains why short calibration windows erode finite-sample coverage. The default operating point therefore lies on a smooth, well-behaved frontier rather than a narrowly tuned optimum, with headline savings stable once \(\varepsilon\) and \(\alpha\) are fixed.

