\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We presented CPS, a conformal shielding layer for client-side ABR. It attaches to an existing ABR controller as a lightweight wrapper, needs no retraining, and can also wrap a learned policy. CPS combines three parts: bandwidth banking at the VMAF knee, an online conformal throughput lower bound with finite-sample coverage under residual exchangeability, and a certified buffer-feasibility test.
We evaluated CPS on \(\CPSepisodes\) paired \mbox{5G} episodes from twelve titles with diverse ladders. Relative to the safety-only conformal projection, banking lowered delivered bitrate by 3.8--4.5\% and rebuffering by 8.9--9.3\%. These savings held for every non-learned host to which CPS was applied, from greedy to RobustMPC. Mean VMAF stayed inside the tolerance \(\pm\CPSepsilon\) VMAF points. Empirical coverage of the throughput lower bound was \(\CPSGreedyCov\), against a target of \(\CPScovTarget\), including warm-up chunks that used the fallback scale.
On the primary \mbox{5G} suite, conformal safety cut mean greedy rebuffering from \(\CPSGreedyRawReb\)\,s to \(\CPSGreedySafReb\)\,s per \(\sim\!192\,\mathrm{s}\) episode. Certified banking trimmed it further to \(\CPSGreedyCertReb\)\,s. Those gains accrued where session-mean ladders saturated at the top and the host requested those rungs.
Optional CPS-P and static budget sweeps (Sections~\ref{sec:eval:improved} and~\ref{sec:eval:sensitivity}) showed that widening \(\varepsilon\) can deepen bitrate savings.
At a comparable bitrate saving, static \(\varepsilon{=}2\) cut greedy rebuffering more than CPS-P (30.8\% vs.\ 12.6\%) at a slightly smaller mean VMAF cost; CPS-P's selective widening showed no measurable advantage on these metrics.
Two regimes appeared. Banking supplied the savings while the ladder knee lay inside the observable operating range. Under broadband stress, the knee moved beyond that range, and the feasibility projection accounted for the stall reduction relative to raw greedy.
A single-seed experiment co-trained PPO with CPS in the loop (Section~\ref{sec:eval:codesign}). It gained \(+\CPScoDVM\) VMAF at \(+\CPScoDReb\)\,s of rebuffering, with a QoE crossover weight \(w^{\star}{=}\CPScoWstar\).
Spare perceptual quality therefore becomes a budget that the client can measure and spend under a finite-sample guarantee.

\paragraph{Limitations and future work}
\label{sec:limits}
Coverage holds at the \(1{-}\alpha\) level when calibration residuals are exchangeable (Section~\ref{sec:method:conformal}).
The present evaluation used a chunk-level simulator without transport-stack or CDN dynamics~\cite{narayanan2021variegated}, a synthetic \mbox{5G} suite, real FCC and Norway/Nornet broadband traces~\cite{fcc2015mba,kvalbein2014}, twelve public titles~\cite{blenderfilms,xiphderf}, a VMAF QoE surrogate~\cite{seufert2015survey}, a TOST margin equal to \(\varepsilon{=}\CPSepsilon\)~\cite{menon2022perceptual,lakens2017equivalence}, and one co-design training seed per policy (\tabref{tab:cps:codesign}).
Empirical coverage was \(\CPSbbCov\) on the 27 broadband traces at \(\alpha{=}\CPSalpha\) and \(0.945\) vs.\ \(0.95\) on the primary \mbox{5G} suite at \(\alpha{=}0.05\) (\tabref{tab:abl:alpha}); savings scaled with ladder saturation and host aggressiveness (\tabref{tab:ladder:spacing}; Section~\ref{sec:eval:main}).
These bounds motivate the next steps: deployment in a production player such as \texttt{dash.js}~\cite{spiteri2018dashjs}, weighted conformal updates under transport-layer (TCP/QUIC) drift~\cite{barber2023beyond,angelopoulos2024online}, multi-seed RL co-training, packet-level or field \mbox{5G} evaluation, Comyco-style learned hosts, Conditional Value-at-Risk (CVaR) objectives~\cite{rockafellar2000}, and telemetry-driven \(\varepsilon\) and \(\alpha\).
The feasibility check must also use the player's delayed buffer signal inside a soft real-time budget beside the ABR loop; the chunk-level micro-benchmark is \(\sim\!130\,\mu\mathrm{s}\) (median).

